WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.330
Welcome to HeatWave Audit Center. Source left, controls in the centre,

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:09.200
audit history right. This walkthrough uses illustrative demo data.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.175
This demonstration explains the current application and its limits. Oracle styling

00:00:15.175 --> 00:00:19.751
does not imply Oracle authorship or endorsement. The security audit extension

00:00:19.751 --> 00:00:23.079
shown later is not implemented in this application.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.776
The web application runs on Oracle Linux nine. Debezium and the

00:00:34.776 --> 00:00:38.951
history writer run on O C I Container Instances. Debezium reads

00:00:38.951 --> 00:00:43.127
source changes, publishes to O C I Streaming, and the writer

00:00:43.127 --> 00:00:47.302
commits history to the target. Vault holds credentials. Resource access stays

00:00:47.302 --> 00:00:49.200
in the virtual machine compartment.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.169
Begin with a demo orders table and a primary key. Creating

00:00:54.169 --> 00:00:57.738
this table prepares the source before registration. Because capture starts later,

00:00:57.738 --> 00:01:01.307
its first schema observation is a baseline. It is not a

00:01:01.307 --> 00:01:04.876
claim that the earlier create statement was captured from the binary

00:01:04.876 --> 00:01:05.200
log.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.847
Register the source and target connections. The Test connection button checks

00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:15.093
MySQL access using a Vault secret reference. Table mappings load schemas

00:01:15.093 --> 00:01:19.340
and tables from those connections. Select one registration and choose Edit

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.200
to change its available settings. Existing capture identities remain protected.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.924
Create or select a stream pool, a data stream, and a

00:01:28.924 --> 00:01:33.249
Connect configuration. Add a queue connection using principal authentication. The mapping

00:01:33.249 --> 00:01:37.573
uses the data stream; its separate schema stream must also be

00:01:37.573 --> 00:01:41.111
ready. This demonstration uses O C I Streaming throughout.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.550
A new flow initialized at End captures schema and future changes,

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:52.500
without copying existing rows. Restarts resume saved offsets. Verify messages and

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.200
target events, not just container status.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.857
Insert order ten forty two. Debezium represents the insert as a

00:02:00.857 --> 00:02:05.114
create event. Its before image is null; its after image contains

00:02:05.114 --> 00:02:09.370
the new row. The writer stores the key, source position, queue

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:13.627
offset and images. A successful target commit must precede acknowledgement of

00:02:13.627 --> 00:02:14.401
the message.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.522
Now change the order status and amount. The same key links

00:02:20.522 --> 00:02:24.444
the earlier and later versions. Select the update in Change history,

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:28.366
then Compare images. The before image shows new and eighty; the

00:02:28.366 --> 00:02:32.288
after image shows paid and ninety nine point ninety five. Full

00:02:32.288 --> 00:02:34.784
row images require the correct source settings.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.619
Delete the source row. It disappears from the current source table,

00:02:42.619 --> 00:02:46.638
but the audit record retains its final before image. The after

00:02:46.638 --> 00:02:50.658
image is null. History remains available for the original insert, the

00:02:50.658 --> 00:02:54.677
update and the deletion. Deleting source data does not erase audit

00:02:54.677 --> 00:02:55.042
history.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:00.776
Search with the typed record key, id ten forty two, and

00:03:00.776 --> 00:03:04.951
a retained date range. The application computes the same keyed fingerprint

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:09.127
from the source identity, schema, table and key. An indexed lookup

00:03:09.127 --> 00:03:13.302
finds that row’s changes. Separate image fingerprints describe content changes; the

00:03:13.302 --> 00:03:15.200
event identity supports replay deduplication.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:21.110
Add a region column with a default, and widen the amount

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:25.620
type. Schema events use the separate schema stream. The target stores

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.130
the prior definition, new definition and a semantic difference. Here, SQL

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:34.640
inspection shows the new column and decimal precision change. Defaults, indexes

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.150
and partitions can only be compared when the captured metadata contains

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.200
them. Unknown properties stay unknown.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.206
Create a locked demonstration user, grant select, then revoke it. These

00:03:47.206 --> 00:03:51.812
are account and privilege operations, not row updates. This application does

00:03:51.812 --> 00:03:56.418
not yet collect them as a security history. Use the MySQL

00:03:56.418 --> 00:04:01.024
Enterprise Audit plugin with reviewed filters and a separate collector. Retain

00:04:01.024 --> 00:04:05.630
the actor, account, scope, action, outcome and timestamp. Do not infer

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:08.561
complete privilege history from row change capture.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.692
The Audit Center opens with all databases. A pie chart compares

00:04:14.692 --> 00:04:18.784
event counts. Choose a database slice or its legend entry to

00:04:18.784 --> 00:04:22.876
see registered table bars, then select a table to drill into

00:04:22.876 --> 00:04:26.968
changes over time. These overview counts are a separate sample dataset,

00:04:26.968 --> 00:04:29.200
not the three earlier demonstration transactions.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.510
Choose a custom date range and an interval of minute, hour,

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:38.420
day or week. Minute charts cover one hour. Switch between line,

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:42.330
bar and stacked bar, and use the operation legend. Counts include

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:46.240
snapshots unless you filter them out. Download a chart image or

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.017
export the loaded table data.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:55.105
Capture row images, compare schemas, and explore history and charts. Security

00:04:55.105 --> 00:04:59.200
auditing needs a separate collector. This was an illustrative replay.
